    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "974bf1e1_c3ce273b",
        "filename": "power/common.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 889,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-09T02:35:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It still feels very wrong that the timeout and transitions are handled in common code even though they do not really apply to all platforms using this common driver. I think it would make sense to just pass ec_params_host_sleep_event_v1 and ec_response_host_sleep_event_v1 into power_chipset_handle_host_sleep_event and let it handle those as the platform implementations like. I understand that you are worried about including ec_commands.h in power.h since it is included by other files too, but it shouldn\u0027t be too bad. We can always forward declare these two structs, but I think it is just fine to just include ec_commands.h directly in power.h",
      "revId": "253c752214d1b9c2b8878431940b26a6acc178da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "94cb06ae_87634e5b",
        "filename": "power/common.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 889,
      "author": {
        "id": 1260470
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-11T16:30:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I did this this way because I consider this a generic facility that I anticipate being ported to other sleep signals. I wanted common handling of these protocol fields so we\u0027d have confidence in their values when they did finally get wired up. This also seems consistent with the style I see throughout the rest of the EC code, where protocol differences are handled at the top level command handlers, and individual values are passed to the more low-level functions.",
      "parentUuid": "974bf1e1_c3ce273b",
      "revId": "253c752214d1b9c2b8878431940b26a6acc178da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b6f53823_b3dcb54d",
        "filename": "power/intel_x86.c",
